      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t we do this the other way around ? Normally A includes B if it uses it. Building libs by what their users do is extremely confusing.\n\nIf lib-bpf does not need struct, then it should not include it and it\u0027s the end of the story.\n\nBut I think it does need it, right ?\nYou can then adopt one of two policies :\n• Link dynamically against lib-struct, and require that any user of lib-bpf embark lib-struct, instead of having lib-bpf-minus-struct for those who do. It\u0027s a LOT less confusing than this scheme.\n• Have lib-bpf-with-dynamically-linked-struct and lib-bpf-with-statically-linked-struct. Then users can choose. IIUC it\u0027s the same thing you are doing here but it\u0027s a lot less confusing than \"minus struct\" (after all the lib is not minus struct, it\u0027s linked against struct dynamically)",
